        <div id="quiet-hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Campground Quiet Hours and the 60 dB Rule
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Campground Generator Hours</h3>

          <p>
            Most campground generator arguments are not about watts, they are about noise. National parks, state parks and many private campgrounds publish generator hours, commonly a morning window and an evening window with nothing overnight, and a growing number also publish a decibel limit. The figure that turns up again and again is around 60 dB measured at 50 feet. Some parks set it lower, some ban generators in certain loops altogether, and a few tent-only areas ban them outright. Check the specific park's rules before the trip, not after the ranger knocks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Noise Ratings Are Measured</h3>

          <p>
            The important detail is how manufacturers rate noise. Almost every dBA figure on a spec sheet is measured at 23 feet, often at a quarter load, often in eco mode. A camping generator rated at 52 dBA at 23 feet will be quieter at 50 feet, which is good news, but the same generator at full load with eco mode off can be 8 to 10 dB louder than its headline figure. Every 10 dB increase is perceived as roughly twice as loud. A unit that complies running a fan and phone chargers may not comply making coffee.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Inverters Dominate Camping</h3>

          <p>
            That is why inverter generators dominate camping. An inverter lets the engine throttle down to match the load, so at 300 watts it idles rather than screaming at a fixed 3,600 rpm. A conventional open-frame generator runs at full speed whatever you plug in and typically lands in the high 60s or 70s at 23 feet, well over a 60 dB limit. If you are still deciding which type to buy, our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter generator vs regular generator</a> guide goes through the trade-offs in detail. For camping, the short version is that an open-frame unit belongs on a job site or in a driveway, not in a campground loop.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Placement and Courtesy</h3>

          <p>
            Placement matters almost as much as the rating. Point the exhaust away from your neighbours and your own tent, keep the generator on the ground rather than a hollow truck bed that acts like a drum, and use the full length of your extension cord. Doubling the distance from a noise source drops the level by about 6 dB, so moving the generator from 10 feet behind your trailer to 20 feet is a real difference to the site next door.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, quiet hours are about courtesy as much as rules. A camping generator that is legal at 50 feet still annoys people at 30 feet in a tight loop. Run it during the published window, charge batteries while it runs, and shut it down when you are done. If you need power through the night for a CPAP or a fridge, that is exactly the job a battery does better, which we come back to below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy for the dB rating at the load you will actually run, not the eco-mode headline.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Spec sheets measure at 23 feet and light load. If the park caps you at 60 dB at 50 feet, a quality inverter rated in the low 50s will usually comply at camp loads. An open-frame generator will not.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Many Watts a Campsite Really Needs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Most Camping Loads Are Small</h3>

          <p>
            Most camping loads are small, which is why the sweet spot for portable camping generators is 1,000 to 2,500 watts. A trailer's converter charging the house battery draws a few hundred watts. LED lights, a vent fan, a furnace blower and phone chargers add another couple of hundred. A CPAP machine without its heated humidifier draws well under 100 watts. Even with all of that running, many campsites sit under 600 watts for most of the day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Loads That Heat or Cool Change the Answer</h3>

          <p>
            The loads that change the answer are anything that heats or cools. A drip coffee maker pulls 800 to 1,200 watts. An electric kettle, hair dryer or small microwave pulls 1,000 to 1,500. A 13,500 BTU rooftop air conditioner runs at roughly 1,200 to 1,600 watts but needs a much bigger surge to start the compressor, often well over 2,000 watts for a fraction of a second. That starting surge, not the running load, is what trips small generators. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical running and starting figures for the appliances people bring camping.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Sizing Logic</h3>

          <p>
            Here is the practical sizing logic. If your list is lights, fans, phones, laptops and a CPAP, a 1,000 watt class generator such as the EU1000i is enough and is the quietest and lightest choice. If you want a coffee maker or a microwave, step up to an 1,800 to 1,900 running watt unit such as the EU2200i or iGen2550c, and do not run the heating appliance at the same time as the converter is bulk-charging. If you want air conditioning, you need either about 3,000 running watts in one box or two paralleled small units.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not buy more generator than you need. A 3,500 watt unit running a 400 watt campsite load is heavier, louder and thirstier than a 2,200 watt unit at the same load, and it gains you nothing between meals. The bigger engine also costs more at the store and takes up more room in the truck. Size for the largest single thing you will run plus what is running with it, add a little headroom, and stop there.
          </p>

