        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Weight: What You Can Actually Lift Into a Truck
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Weight Gets Ignored</h3>

          <p>
            Every camping generator spec sheet lists a weight, and it is the number buyers most often ignore. A generator that cannot be lifted by the person who will be lifting it ends up left at home. The practical ceiling for one adult lifting over a pickup tailgate or into an SUV cargo area is around 45 to 50 lb. That is why the 2,000 to 2,500 watt inverter class, which mostly lands between 40 and 50 lb, is the core of the camping market.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dry Weight vs Real Weight</h3>

          <p>
            Look closely at what weight a listing quotes. Dry weight excludes oil and fuel. A gallon of gasoline adds about 6 lb and the oil adds a little more, so a 46.5 lb dry generator is closer to 53 lb when it is ready to run. Some brands list shipping weight, which includes packaging and is higher still. Compare like with like, and when a retailer and the manufacturer disagree, which they often do, trust the manufacturer's dry weight and add fuel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Changes Above 50 Pounds</h3>

          <p>
            Above 50 lb the design changes. The 3,500 to 4,500 watt inverters that can run a camper air conditioner, such as the Champion 200988, weigh in the region of 90 to 110 lb and ride on wheels with a telescoping handle. They roll easily across a gravel pad, but getting them into a truck bed takes two people or a ramp. If your camping style means a generator travels in the bed of a pickup and lives on the ground at the site, that is fine. If it rides in the back of an SUV, it usually is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for Two Small Units</h3>

          <p>
            This is also the strongest argument for buying two small units instead of one large one. Two 45 lb generators are two easy lifts. One 100 lb generator is one hard one. We cover the parallel option in the next section, but the weight logic is worth stating on its own: for a camper who sometimes needs air conditioning and usually does not, splitting the power across two light units is often the better buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transporting a Generator Safely</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, think about where the generator rides. Gasoline generators should travel upright with the fuel valve off, and many owners run the carburettor dry before a long drive so fumes do not fill the vehicle. A sealed truck toolbox or an exterior cargo carrier keeps the smell out of the cab. Weight matters for that too: hitch-mounted cargo carriers have tongue-weight limits, and a heavy generator on a long carrier can exceed them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parallel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Parallel Kits: Two Small Generators vs One Big One
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Parallel Kits Work</h3>

          <p>
            Most 2,000 to 2,500 watt inverter generators can be linked to a second, matching unit with a parallel kit. The kit combines their output into one outlet panel, usually with a TT-30R 30 amp RV outlet, so two units rated at 1,800 or 1,900 running watts deliver roughly 3,600 to 3,800 running watts together. That is enough to start and run most 13,500 BTU camper air conditioners alongside the rest of the trailer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Appeal: Flexibility</h3>

          <p>
            The appeal is flexibility. For most of a trip you run one generator, which is quieter and burns less fuel, and you only bring the second online for the hottest part of the afternoon or when you need to run the microwave and the air conditioner together. Each unit stays light enough to lift. And if one ever needs repair, you still have half your power. For families who camp in the summer and also want a little home backup in winter, two small units cover both jobs better than one big one does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Downsides: Cost and Compatibility</h3>

          <p>
            The downsides are cost and compatibility. Two good inverter generators cost more than one 3,500 watt unit, and you need the parallel cable or kit on top. Parallel kits generally only work between matching models or within the same brand. Honda pairs with Honda, Westinghouse with Westinghouse, Champion with Champion, and even within a brand some models lack the port. Check that both units list parallel capability and buy the brand's own kit rather than an unbranded one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When One Larger Inverter Is Simpler</h3>

          <p>
            If you are certain you will run air conditioning every single day, one larger inverter like the Champion 200988 is the simpler answer: one engine to maintain, one fuel tank to fill, one thing to set up. If air conditioning is occasional, start with one small inverter and add the second later. That path is cheaper up front and leaves you with the lighter, quieter generator for the eighty percent of nights when you do not need cooling. Our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">best RV generator</a> guide covers the larger single-unit options in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rv-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Running a Camper Air Conditioner From a Generator
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Camper Air Conditioner Draws</h3>

          <p>
            The air conditioner is the load that decides whether a camping generator is big enough. A 13,500 BTU rooftop unit typically runs at 1,200 to 1,600 watts once it is going, which looks comfortable for an 1,800 watt generator. The problem is the compressor's starting surge, which can be several times the running draw for an instant. Many 2,200 watt generators will start it on a cool day with nothing else running and fail on a hot day with the converter charging. Owners commonly report exactly that pattern.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Ways to Make It Work</h3>

          <p>
            There are three ways to make it work. The first is a single generator with about 3,000 running watts or more, such as the Champion 200988, which handles the surge and the rest of the trailer with room to spare. The second is two paralleled small units, discussed above. The third is a soft starter fitted to the air conditioner, a small module that ramps the compressor up gradually and cuts the surge sharply. Many owners report that a soft starter lets a single 2,200 watt inverter run a 13,500 BTU unit, though results depend on the air conditioner, the heat and altitude.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Altitude Costs Power</h3>

          <p>
            Altitude deserves a mention because so much camping happens in the mountains. Engines lose power as the air thins, and a common rule of thumb is roughly 3 to 3.5 percent of output per 1,000 feet above sea level. A generator that just starts your air conditioner at the coast may not at 7,000 feet. Some manufacturers sell high-altitude jets for their carburettors; check the manual if you camp high regularly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manage the Load</h3>

          <p>
            Whichever route you choose, manage the load. Let the air conditioner start before you turn on the microwave, keep the converter's charge rate in mind, and do not run the water heater on electric while cooling. A generator running at 90 percent of its rating all afternoon is loud, thirsty and close to tripping. One running at 60 percent is quieter and will last longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fuel, Runtime and Carrying Gas Safely
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Quarter-Load Runtime Figures</h3>

          <p>
            Runtime figures on camping generators are always quoted at a quarter load, because that is the flattering number. The Honda EU2200i is rated for 8.1 hours at a quarter load but 3.2 hours at full load on its 0.95 gallon tank. The Honda EU1000i is listed at 6.8 hours at a quarter load and 3.0 at full load. In practice a campsite generator spends most of its time at light load, so the long figure is closer to reality for a lights-and-charging evening, but plan on the short one if you are running a coffee maker and a microwave.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weekend Fuel Arithmetic</h3>

          <p>
            For a weekend trip, the arithmetic is simple. Two evenings and two mornings of light use on a 2,200 watt inverter is often well under two gallons of gasoline, which fits in one small fuel can. A week of daily air conditioning on a 3,500 watt unit is a different story and can mean several gallons a day. Know your daily consumption before you leave, because buying gas in remote places is not always possible and is never cheap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carrying Gasoline Safely</h3>

          <p>
            Carry fuel in an approved container, upright, outside the passenger area if you can, and keep it out of direct sun. Use fresh, ethanol-free gasoline where you can find it or add stabiliser, because a camping generator often sits for weeks between trips and stale fuel is the most common reason a small engine will not start. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/">storing a portable generator</a> covers the off-season routine that prevents gummed carburettors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane as the Alternative</h3>

          <p>
            Dual fuel units like the Champion 201323 and 200988 avoid much of this by running on propane, the same fuel many campers already carry for a stove or grill. Propane does not go stale and does not spill. The trade-off is lower output on propane and shorter runtime per pound than you might expect, because a 20 lb cylinder holds about 4.6 gallons of propane, which carries less energy than the same volume of gasoline. If you are mostly interested in propane, our <a href="/best-propane-generator/">best propane generator</a> guide goes deeper on runtime math and cold-weather behaviour.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rated Runtime, Light Load</p>
