        <div id="power-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When a Power Station Is the Better Camping Buy
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for a Power Station</h3>

          <p>
            A fuel generator is not always the right camping answer, and for a lot of campers it is the wrong one. A portable power station, a large lithium battery with an inverter and outlets, is silent, has no exhaust, can run all night inside a tent or trailer, and is allowed during quiet hours. For a CPAP, phones, lights, a fan and a 12V fridge over a weekend, a 500 to 1,000Wh power station does the job with no noise at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for a Fuel Generator</h3>

          <p>
            The case for a fuel generator starts where batteries run out. A gallon of gasoline holds vastly more energy than any battery a camper can lift, so for a week off-grid without solar, or for anything that heats or cools, a generator still wins on cost and capacity. A 1,000Wh power station can run a 1,200 watt coffee maker for well under an hour of brewing and would be flattened by a camper air conditioner in less than an hour. A 2,200 watt inverter runs the air conditioner, with help, for as long as you keep feeding it fuel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Many Campers Own Both</h3>

          <p>
            Many experienced campers end up with both: a power station for overnight and quiet hours, charged by solar or by the generator during the permitted window. That combination keeps generator runtime short, keeps you within the rules, and means the generator does the heavy lifting only when it needs to. If your loads are all small, skip the generator and read our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power station for camping</a> guide instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the broader trade-offs, including running costs and indoor safety, see our <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">portable power station vs generator</a> comparison. The honest summary for camping: buy a power station if you never run anything that heats or cools and you have sunshine or short trips. Buy a camping generator if you need air conditioning, a microwave, or a week without shore power or sun.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Carbon Monoxide, Placement and Campsite Safety
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carbon Monoxide Risk at Campsites</h3>

          <p>
            Every fuel-burning camping generator produces carbon monoxide, and campsites create some of the riskiest situations for it: tents and trailers are small, sleeping people do not notice symptoms, and generators get placed close to save cord length. Never run a generator inside a tent, trailer, vehicle, screen room or under an awning. Place it downwind where possible, with the exhaust pointing away from every opening, including your neighbour's.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Insist on Automatic CO Shutdown</h3>

          <p>
            Generators with automatic CO shutdown are now the norm and worth insisting on. Honda's CO-MINDER, Westinghouse's CO sensor and Champion's CO Shield all shut the engine down when carbon monoxide builds up around the unit. They are a backstop, not permission to run a generator near a tent. You still need a working CO alarm inside your camper or tent, and most RVs ship with one that should be tested before each season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rain, Wet Ground and Refuelling</h3>

          <p>
            Rain and wet ground are the other campsite hazard. Keep the generator dry and on firm, level ground, ideally under a purpose-made generator cover that leaves the exhaust and cooling vents open. Do not run cords through standing water, and use outdoor-rated extension cords sized for the load. Our guide to <a href="/can-you-run-a-generator-in-the-rain/">running a generator in the rain</a> covers covers and cords in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, let the engine cool before refuelling, never refuel with it running, and store fuel away from the generator and the campfire. These are basic rules, but campsites are exactly where people relax and skip them. Most generator burns and fires happen during refuelling, often at dusk when people are rushing to get the lights back on.
          </p>
        </div>

