        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For lights, fans, phones, laptops and a CPAP, a 1,000 watt class inverter is enough. To add a coffee maker, microwave or hair dryer, step up to an 1,800 to 1,900 running watt unit such as the Honda EU2200i or Westinghouse iGen2550c. To run a 13,500 BTU camper air conditioner, plan on roughly 3,000 running watts, either in one generator or two paralleled small units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How quiet does a camping generator need to be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many campgrounds cap generator noise at around 60 dB measured at 50 feet, and some set lower limits or ban generators in certain loops. Manufacturers measure at 23 feet, so a quality inverter rated in the low 50s at 23 feet will usually comply at light camp loads. Open-frame generators rated in the high 60s or 70s usually will not.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 2,200 watt generator run an RV air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. A 13,500 BTU unit runs at roughly 1,200 to 1,600 watts but needs a larger surge to start. Owners commonly report success on cool days with nothing else running and failure on hot days or at altitude. Adding a soft starter to the air conditioner, or paralleling a second generator, makes it reliable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an inverter generator better for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, almost always. An inverter throttles the engine to match the load, which makes it far quieter at typical campsite loads, more fuel efficient, and produces clean power for laptops and CPAP machines. Inverters are also lighter for their output. Conventional generators are cheaper per watt but are usually too loud for campground rules.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I get a dual fuel camping generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you already carry propane for a stove, a dual fuel unit such as the Champion 201323 lets you skip carrying gasoline, and propane does not go stale between trips. Expect less output on propane and plan runtime from the propane cylinder size. If you camp in cold weather, keep gasoline as a backup because small propane cylinders struggle in the cold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a portable power station better than a generator for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For small loads, often yes: it is silent, has no exhaust, can run overnight during quiet hours and can be used inside a tent or trailer. It cannot match a generator for anything that heats or cools, or for a week off-grid without sun. Many campers use both, charging the battery during permitted generator hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a camping generator in the back of my truck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is better not to. A hollow truck bed amplifies vibration and noise, and exhaust can collect under a cap or tonneau cover and drift into the cab. Set the generator on the ground, on a level surface, at least 20 feet from tents and trailers with the exhaust pointing away from people and openings.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an open-frame generator for a campground</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Open-frame generators run at a fixed high engine speed and are typically rated well into the high 60s or 70s dBA at 23 feet. That is far over the roughly 60 dB at 50 feet many parks allow. They are cheaper per watt, but they belong at a job site or home, and you may be told to switch one off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the eco-mode noise figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The headline dBA on a camping generator is almost always at quarter load in eco mode. Run a coffee maker or an air conditioner and the same unit can be several decibels louder, perceived as noticeably louder. Compare noise ratings at similar loads where the manufacturer publishes them, and assume the real figure at your load is higher.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the air conditioner's starting surge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A camper air conditioner may run at 1,200 to 1,600 watts but needs a much bigger surge to start. A 2,200 watt generator that copes on a mild evening can trip on a hot afternoon at altitude. Plan for a soft starter, a parallel kit or a 3,500 running watt unit if cooling is non-negotiable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying more weight than you can lift</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 100 lb generator that needs two people and a ramp often stays in the garage. Check dry weight, add about 6 lb per gallon of fuel, and be honest about who will load it. For most campers, two light units beat one heavy one, and one light unit beats a heavy one left behind.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the CO alarm because the generator has shutoff</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Automatic CO shutdown protects people standing near the generator, not people asleep in a tent or trailer twenty feet downwind. Keep a battery-powered CO alarm inside your sleeping space, test it before each trip, and never place the generator where exhaust can drift toward a door, window or tent wall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

