        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most campers the right portable generator for camping is a 2,000 to 2,500 watt inverter that weighs under 50 lb and carries a noise rating in the low 50s. The Honda EU2200i is the one to buy if you want it to last, the Westinghouse iGen2550c is the one to buy if you want the most for your money, and the Champion 201323 is the one to buy if you would rather burn propane than carry gas.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Do not buy more generator than your campsite needs, and be honest about whether you need one at all. If your loads are lights, phones and a CPAP, a power station is quieter, simpler and allowed all night. If you need air conditioning, plan for the starting surge before you buy, not after the first hot afternoon. And whichever you choose, read the park's generator rules before you pack it.
          </p>
